		{
			"id": "114140292827906268",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://lea.lgbt/@lea/114140292780861426",
			"author": {
				"name": "Lea Rosema",
				"url": "https://lea.lgbt/@lea",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/966/669/846/784/668/original/f71cb78e4f341a20.jpeg"
			},
			"date": "2025-03-10T21:37:31.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> additionally, I would also argue: baseline is still an assumption about which features are available to your users. A good example for that is maybe a book store. Not everybody will update their e-reader. Baseline features might not be available on the e-reader device. The book site might look like a mess there.</p><p>Baseline won't safe you; you still need to investigate how users use a site. But that would require to track users. Better: Progressive enhancement by default. 💜</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
